The original does a linear scan over all message definitions on every call. This version builds a 65536-entry index table (128 KB) the first time the function runs. After that, each lookup is a single table read.

- **Same results:** the table is filled in reverse order, so when two entries share a number, the first one in `mesgs` wins, exactly as in the linear scan. Unknown numbers still return `NULL`.
- **Safe to share:** the table is a function-local static built only from the constant `mesgs` array. C++11 guarantees its initialization is thread-safe, and it never changes afterwards, so it does not depend on any global mutable state.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        Index()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
                idx[(FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
    };
    static const Index table;

    const FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```